Portuguese law does not mention hitchhiking (''pedir boleia''), so it is legal on ordinary roads and the restriction is a road-type one. [https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/legislacao-consolidada/lei/2013-116041830-116043213 Article 72.º of the ''Código da Estrada''] prohibits pedestrian traffic on ''autoestradas'' '''and on their access roads''' where these are signposted, and article 75.º extends the same rule to ''vias reservadas a automóveis e motociclos'' — so the slip road is out as well as the carriageway. Article 72.º(2)(b) forbids stopping or parking "even off the carriageway" other than in places specially designated for it, which rules out the hard shoulder for the driver too; breaking either carries a fine of €120–600, or €250–1250 for stopping on the carriageway itself. That leaves the ''áreas de serviço'' as the one place on the motorway where a driver may legally stop and you may legally stand. Thumb from the on-ramp before the signs, and on ''estradas nacionais'' hitchhike freely from the verge.
